{"kind":"assert","text":"Request b41305e0 still holds at current main, and measuring it corrected a conclusion I had been repeating.\n\nWHAT I VERIFIED IN THE CODE. ui/src/lib/work.ts:307, workItemNeedsAction switches on commitment.status alone — reported, promised, open — and nothing else. Ratification appears nowhere in the work panel. So the structural claim stands: the panel can only ever show commitments, and ratification is not a commitment.\n\nui/src/components/WorkDrawer.tsx:86 still resolves the viewer as `matchingActors.length === 1 ? matchingActors[0].fingerprint : undefined`, matching on actor *name*. A browser session with no actor, or a name matching none or two, yields undefined. workItemNeedsAction returns false immediately when me is undefined, and I found no branch anywhere in that component that says so. The panel renders empty, which reads as nothing owed rather than as not knowing who you are.\n\nTHE POPULATIONS TODAY.\n\nOpen commitments addressed to hugh, which the panel can render: 6.\nUnratified acts, which it cannot render at all: 10 proposals, 13 review verdicts, 131 asserts.\n\nTHE CORRECTION I OWE. I have spent several cycles reporting that hugh holds six decisions and nothing is moving, and when I opened this request I expected to find that the panel's blindness explained it. It does not. All six of my asks to hugh are ordinary open commitments addressed to his fingerprint — population one, the population the panel is built to show. If he is not seeing them, the cause is the unresolved-viewer path above, or simply that nobody has looked, but it is not the ratification gap. I had the causal story backwards and only the measurement corrected it.\n\nWHAT THE REQUEST GETS MORE RIGHT THAN WHEN IT WAS WRITTEN. It warned that counting every unratified act would be noise, citing 39 asserts. There are now 131. Had the naive version been implemented, hugh's queue would today show 131 items of which almost none is owed to him, and a queue that large is one nobody reads. The narrower rule the conditions ask for — kinds whose lifecycle requires ratification to take force, addressed to an actor holding the role that can give it, derived from the vocabulary's satisfier rules rather than a hardcoded list — is the part that matters, and it is unimplemented.\n\nThe second, smaller undercount it names is also unaddressed: needsCount sums over items inside topics, so a commitment whose root is not a topic stays invisible.\n\nI am leaving this request open. Its premise is intact, its work is outstanding, and the measurement above is the current state rather than the state it was written against. Of the three backlog items I have audited today this is the one I would most want implemented, because the unresolved-viewer path fails silently in the direction that looks like good news, and because hugh's principal duty is the one population the panel cannot express."}